{#
  module/Books/view/books/library-imports/create.phtml — start a spreadsheet import.

  The original asked for two things a librarian cannot know: a **path on the server**
  and the **exact name of a worksheet**, both as free text, with no way to put a file on
  the server in the first place. Import 14, still pending from 2021, records what that
  produced — `C:\Users\Ramon Vergara\Desktop\intento.xlsx`, someone's own desktop, which
  is the only path a person has.

  So the page now leads with the template download, because the first question is "what
  should the spreadsheet look like", and takes an upload for the second. The worksheet
  question has moved to the next step, where the file has been read and the names can be
  offered as a list.

  The column table is the same information the original's `<dl>` carried, with two
  differences: the headings are the ones the template writes rather than one library's
  Spanish, and each says what it means. It stays on the page rather than moving wholly
  into the file, because a librarian deciding whether to bother is here, not in Excel.
#}
{% extends 'layout.html.twig' %}

{% block content %}
<h1>{{ translate('Begin new import') }}</h1>

<div class="panel panel-default">
  <div class="panel-heading"><strong>{{ translate('Do you have a spreadsheet yet?') }}</strong></div>
  <div class="panel-body">
    <p>{{ translate('Download one of these, fill it in, and upload it below.') }}</p>
    <p>
      <a class="btn btn-default" href="{{ template_url }}">{{ translate('Blank template') }}</a>
      <a class="btn btn-default" href="{{ export_url }}">{{ translate('This library\'s books') }}</a>
    </p>
    <p class="help-block">{{ translate('The blank template is for adding books. The export holds every active book in this library, in the same columns, and is the one to use for correcting records in bulk — or for a complete import.') }}</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>{{ translate('How an import works') }}</h2>
<ol>
  <li>{{ translate('Upload the file here. Nothing is read from it yet.') }}</li>
  <li>{{ translate('Check which spreadsheet column feeds which field. The columns are matched for you; you can change any of them.') }}</li>
  <li>{{ translate('Look at what the import would do — how many books it would add, change and retire.') }}</li>
  <li>{{ translate('Confirm. Only then is anything written.') }}</li>
</ol>

<h2>{{ translate('The columns') }}</h2>
<p>{{ translate('The first row of the sheet holds the headings. Only Barcode and Title are required, and a heading the import does not recognise is listed for you rather than ignored silently.') }}</p>
<table class="table table-condensed">
<thead>
<tr>
  <th>{{ translate('Column') }}</th>
  <th>{{ translate('Required') }}</th>
  <th>{{ translate('Meaning') }}</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
{% for column in columns %}
<tr>
  <td><strong>{{ column.heading }}</strong></td>
  <td>{{ column.required ? translate('Yes') : '' }}</td>
  <td>{{ column.help is empty ? '' : translate(column.help) }}</td>
</tr>
{% endfor %}
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>{{ translate('Upload') }}</h2>
{{ form_open(form, self_url) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('name')) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('libraryId'), false) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('description')) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('file')) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('isCompleteImport')) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('security')) }}
{{ form_row(form.get('submit')) }}
{{ form_close() }}
{% endblock %}
